  "question": {
    "text": "<p>A particle is oscillating on the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>x</mi></math>-axis with an amplitude <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> about the point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><mi>cm</mi></math> with a frequency. A concave mirror of focal length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math> is placed at the origin (see figure).</p><p><img src=\"assets\\qt0224_9e7d167a3e70.png\" style=\"width: 290px; height: 149px;\" /><br />Identify the correct statements?<br />(i) The image executes periodic motion.<br />(ii) The image executes non-periodic motion.<br />(iii) The turning points of the image are asymmetric with respect to the image of the point at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>&#160;</mi><mi>cm</mi></math>.<br />(iv) The distance between the turning points of the oscillation of the image is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>100</mn><mn>21</mn></mfrac><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#160;</mi><mi>cm</mi></math>.</p>"
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "text": "(ii, iii)",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "(i, iii, iv)",
      "is_correct": true
    },
    {
      "text": "(i, iv)",
      "is_correct": false
    },
    {
      "text": "(ii, iv)",
      "is_correct": false
    }
  ],
  "correct_answer": [
    "B"
  ],
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Recall the proper formula of mirror formula,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>f</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>v</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>u</mi></mfrac></math>, given&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>&nbsp;and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>u</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>&nbsp;then the formula of the concave mirror,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>10</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>v</mi></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>. As,&nbsp;image copies the time period of the object&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced><mi>A</mi></mfenced></math>&nbsp;is right as well. It will be a periodic motion. For one extreme,&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>v</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>40</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>, right arrow.</p><p>For other extreme,&nbsp;</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>12</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>v</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>7</mn><mn>60</mn></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>. These points are asymmetrical about&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>.&nbsp;Now amplitude of oscillation of image,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>&#8658;</mo><mfrac><mn>40</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mn>60</mn><mn>7</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>100</mn><mn>21</mn></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>cm</mi></math>.</p><p>&nbsp;</p>"
  }
}